  "question": "For the function $f(x)=x^3-2x^2+5$, answer the following:<br><br>(a) Obtain the linear approximation of $f$ near $x=2$.<br><br>(b) Use it to approximate $f(2.02)$.<br><br>(c) Calculate the exact value of $f(2.02)$ and hence determine the absolute error and percentage error in the linear approximation.",
  "answeroption": "C",
  "explanation": "<b>(a)</b><br>For $f(x)=x^3-2x^2+5$,<br>$$f'(x)=3x^2-4x$$<br>At $x=2$,<br>$$f(2)=2^3-2(2)^2+5=5$$<br>and<br>$$f'(2)=3(2)^2-4(2)=4$$<br>Therefore, the linear approximation near $x=2$ is<br>$$L(x)=f(2)+f'(2)(x-2)$$<br>$$L(x)=5+4(x-2)$$<br><br><b>(b)</b><br>For $x=2.02$,<br>$$L(2.02)=5+4(0.02)=5.08$$<br>Therefore,<br>$$f(2.02)\\approx 5.08$$<br><br><b>(c)</b><br>The exact value is<br>$$f(2.02)=(2.02)^3-2(2.02)^2+5$$<br>$$f(2.02)=8.242408-8.1608+5=5.081608$$<br>The absolute error is<br>$$|5.081608-5.08|=0.001608$$<br>The percentage error is<br>$$\\frac{0.001608}{5.081608}\\times 100\\approx 0.0316\\%$$",
  "explanation_image": [],
